{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfiehUMVhopvjdjLSb2WX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've rewritten the repair candidate `switch_belief_habitual_side_lapse` with a different parameter layout. It passes the self-check, but whether it converges is still untested, and that is what sank the previous attempt.\n\n**What it refines:** `personal_chance_switch_belief`, the model tied with the best. The one change targets the critique that people's individual rates of choosing Left vary more than the best model produces. On some fraction of trials people skip the judgement and click their own habitual side. This differs from the live `person_side_habit_switch_belief`, whose side bias adds to the evidence and so only matters on close pairs. Here the pull towards the habitual side is a fixed share, even on clear-cut pairs.\n\n**Why it should converge this time:** the earlier attempt had both a lapse rate and a side preference per person. When someone rarely lapses, their side preference has almost no data behind it, so the two traded off against each other. The fix has three parts:\n- **Shared lapse rate:** one lapse rate for everyone, with a tighter prior.\n- **Fixed spread of sides:** each person's habitual side varies around a fitted mean with a fixed spread. With no fitted spread, the poorly informed per-person sides can't form a funnel.\n- **Merged person parameters:** each person's sensitivity and switch belief now enter as one combined weight, as in `person_side_habit_switch_belief`. That removes the trade-off between the two.\n\n**Self-check result:** `OK`. The log-probability is finite, the data contract holds, and the short smoke fit gave a finite ELPD-LOO of −2113.3. That is roughly on par with the incumbent's −2107.3, but the smoke fit is only 100 draws on one chain. The check doesn't test convergence, so whether this geometry clears R-hat ≤ 1.05 and bulk ESS ≥ 100 will only show at admission.\n\n**Two risks before admission:**\n- **Speed:** the smoke fit took 127 s for 200 iterations on a single CPU. If the full fit runs at that rate it could approach the 30-minute limit per sampling run. The rejected attempt had the same structure and failed on convergence rather than time, so I expect it to fit in time.\n- **LOO reliability:** the smoke fit flagged 5.8% of trials with high Pareto k, above the 1% tolerance. A single 100-draw chain is crude, so the full fit may not show this.\n\nThis is the slot's final attempt; a second rejection ends it.\n\nFiles are in `experiment2/model_loop/iter_1/candidate_5_repair_1/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt`\n- `candidate.py`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3703,"cache_read_input_tokens":78368,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3703,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"03e001a3-dba2-4bde-a2c5-4fda7cfb8ee9","uuid":"3e36deaf-b4b6-4e41-85e3-382a86f94c42","timestamp":"2026-10-05T06:54:19.554Z","request_id":"req_011CfiehTLjLFQQtjrAHmehU"}
